       "mime": "x-application/wolfram-language-html"
      },
      {
       "data": "Out[44]= \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\left\\{\\text{\\#solutions}\\to 1,\\text{tc from solver} \\right. \\\\\n  &\\left. \\to \\left\\{\\frac{172}{21},\\frac{279005}{441},-\\frac{6308}{21},\\frac{3940}{63},\\frac{1880}{3}\\right\\} \\right. \\\\\n  &\\left. ,\\text{tc from $\\tau$vac} \\right. \\\\\n  &\\left. \\to \\left\\{\\frac{172}{21},\\frac{279005}{441},-\\frac{6308}{21},\\frac{3940}{63},\\frac{1880}{3}\\right\\} \\right. \\\\\n  &\\left. ,\\text{difference}\\to \\left\\{0,0,0,0,0\\right\\},\\text{residual}\\to 0\\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}",
       "mime": "text/plain"
      }
     ],
     "id": "36ef95a4-7c29-4059-b0bf-d8066e39a8fe"
    }
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 5. Validation on the permuted vacua\n\nThe cleanest test states are the **vacua**: weight `n = π({S+P,S+P,0,0})` for each of the\n`4!/(2!2!) = 6` distinct permutations `π`. Each has weight multiplicity 1, so the solve is unique\nand entirely **rational** — no algebraic numbers, so \"residual = 0\" is an exact statement rather\nthan a numeric one.\n\nEach such state is `v_π ⊗ w_π`, the extreme-weight vector of `π` in both factors, on which the\nmonodromy acts triangularly with `T_ii → ν^π_i(u) = (u-θ1-hπ(λ_1)_i)(u-θ2-hπ(λ_2)_i)`. So the exact\neigenvalue is predicted independently:\n\n$$ \\tau_a^{\\pi}(u) \\;=\\; \\sum_{|I|=a} z_I \\prod_{k=1}^{a} \\nu^{\\pi}_{i_k}\\big(u-(a-k)h\\big), $$\n\nand the solver has to reproduce it — for all five `tc[i]`, for all six `π`, with no fitting.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* The six vacuum weights and their site-weight decomposition. For n = π({S+P,S+P,0,0}) the\n   two sites carry π({S,S,0,0}) and π({P,P,0,0}) — recovered from n by n_i = S+P <-> occupied.\n\n   SHIFT ORDERING. The state v_π (x) w_π is an extreme-weight vector, annihilated by the raising\n   operators of the Borel adapted to π, not of the standard one. So in the principal quantum minor\n   the shifts u-(a-k)h are assigned along the π-adapted order\n\n       ord = Join[A, complement of A],    A = {i : n_i = S+P}   (the occupied directions),\n\n   not along the natural index order. This was determined empirically — searching all 24\n   permutations against the solver's own τ output selects exactly the orders with A first\n   (the order WITHIN each pair is immaterial, since ν is constant on A and on its complement).\n   Consistency: for a=4 the multiset of ν's is (ν_a,ν_a,ν_b,ν_b) in either ordering, so qdet is\n   ordering-independent — as it must be, being central. *)\nClearAll[vacWeights, siteWeights, vacOrder, νgen, τvacP];\nvacWeights[S_, P_] := DeleteDuplicates[Permutations[{S + P, S + P, 0, 0}]];\nsiteWeights[S_, P_, n_List] := {If[# === S + P, S, 0] & /@ n, If[# === S + P, P, 0] & /@ n};\nvacOrder[S_, P_, n_List] := Join[Flatten[Position[n, S + P]], Flatten[Position[n, 0]]];\n\nνgen[l1_, l2_][i_][u_] := (u - θ1 - h l1[[i]]) (u - θ2 - h l2[[i]]);\nτvacP[l1_, l2_, ord_][a_][u_] := Total[\n   Function[I, (Times @@ (z /@ I)) Product[νgen[l1, l2][SortBy[I, Position[ord, #][[1, 1]] &][[k]]][u - (a - k) h], {k, a}]] /@\n    Subsets[Range[4], {a}]];\n\nTable[{n, vacOrder[S, P, n]}, {n, vacWeights[S, P]}] // TableForm",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
